<!-- GUIDE CONTENT -->
<section class="section legal guide">
  <div class="section-inner legal-inner">

    <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
    <h1 class="legal-title">A git worktree app for Mac that moves your whole workspace</h1>
    <p class="legal-updated">Grove for Mac · Updated November 2026</p>

    <div class="legal-callout">
      <strong>Short version:</strong> Grove is a native macOS app that lists every git worktree in your
      repository and lets you switch between them with one click. When you switch, your AI coding agent, shell,
      file tree, diff and git panel all move to that checkout together, so you never end up committing on one
      branch while reviewing another.
    </div>

    <div class="legal-prose">

      <h2>Why developers use git worktrees</h2>
      <p>
        <code>git worktree</code> lets one repository have several working directories at once, each on its own
        branch. You can fix a bug on <code>main</code> while a feature branch keeps building, review a pull request
        without stashing your work, or give each AI coding agent its own branch so two agents never edit the same
        files.
      </p>
      <p>
        The catch is everything around the command. After <code>git worktree add</code> you still have to
        <code>cd</code> into the new folder, open another terminal tab, point your editor and diff tool at it, and
        restart your agent there. Then you have to remember which window belongs to which branch.
      </p>

      <h2>How Grove manages multiple worktrees</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Every worktree in one list.</strong> The Worktrees panel in the sidebar shows all the worktrees
          of the selected repository, including the ones you created on the command line.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Create one from a branch.</strong> Pick an existing branch or type a new one. Grove creates a
          standard git worktree under a folder you choose in Settings, laid out as
          <code>&lt;root&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;/&lt;branch&gt;</code>, so you never have to think about paths.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One click switches everything.</strong> Selecting a worktree points the whole app at that
          checkout: the Agent and Shell panes, the file tree, the Changes diff, and the git panel for branch,
          commit, pull and push.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Each worktree keeps its own state.</strong> The agent you picked, your terminal tabs, pinned
          websites and any pending diff-review comments are remembered per checkout and come back after a relaunch.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Removal is confirmed.</strong> Removing a worktree deletes a checkout from disk, so Grove asks
          first and tells you if you'd lose unsent review comments.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <figure>
        <img src="/screenshots/agent.png" width="1800" height="1125" loading="lazy"
             alt="Grove's sidebar with the workspace list, the Worktrees panel and the git panel, next to Claude Code running in the selected checkout" />
        <figcaption>The Worktrees panel sits in the sidebar. The agent, shell and diff follow whichever checkout is selected.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Parallel branches with AI coding agents</h2>
      <p>
        Worktrees and coding agents fit together well: run Claude Code on a feature branch in one worktree and
        Codex on a bug fix in another, each in its own isolated checkout. The terminal session for each checkout
        keeps running when you switch away, so an agent in the middle of a task isn't interrupted. When it's done,
        open the Changes tab for that worktree and review exactly what it changed.
      </p>
      <p>
        More on the agent side in <a href="/claude-code-mac-app.html">Run Claude Code in a native Mac app</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Real git, no lock-in</h2>
      <p>
        Grove runs the <code>git</code> already installed on your Mac. The worktrees it creates are ordinary git
        worktrees: <code>git worktree list</code> shows them, and you can keep using the command line next to
        Grove. Nothing is stored in a proprietary format, and Grove doesn't force-push or delete branches.
      </p>

      <h2>A native Mac app, not a web wrapper</h2>
      <p>
        Grove is built in Swift and SwiftUI rather than Electron. It follows your system's appearance, opens
        quickly, and keeps your agents and shells running even if you close the window.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <h3>Does Grove work with worktrees I already have?</h3>
      <p>Yes. It reads the repository's worktree list, so existing worktrees show up as soon as you add the repository.</p>
      <h3>Do I have to stop using the git CLI?</h3>
      <p>No. Grove uses standard git under the hood, so the command line, other git clients and Grove all see the same state.</p>
      <h3>What does it cost?</h3>
      <p>
        There's a 7-day free trial. After that, a license is $9.99 one-time for one Mac, with every future update
        included. Grove needs macOS 26 or later.
      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="legal-callout guide-cta">
      <strong>Try Grove for Mac free for 7 days.</strong> Signed and notarized by Apple, no subscription.
      <p><a href="/#download" role="button" class="btn btn-primary">Download Grove</a></p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

</Base>
